 "exaggeration": 0.7,
 "cfgWeight": 0.4,
 "text": "How do you remove a plugin from a running program, without restarting it? In V.S. Code, eighty-seven of the top one hundred extensions run code, and removing one means restarting the whole extension host. Every cache and connection is lost. A new paper splits the problem in two. First, time: when a component leaves, everything it changed must be undone. Then space: components depend on each other, and those dependencies come and go while the program runs. For time, the answer is a revertible effect. Every change a component makes, like adding a command or opening a connection, hands the runtime its inverse. The runtime keeps them in a stack. To remove the component, it runs the stack backwards, and the program is back exactly as it was. For space, the answer is a reactive coeffect. A component declares what it needs, say, a database. It waits, inactive, until a provider shows up, then it activates. If the database goes away, the component deactivates, and its effects are undone too. Both ideas run through one shared context. Every effect and every dependency passes through it, so components can come and go without disturbing each other. The authors built this as Cordis. It already runs Koishi, a chatbot framework with over four thousand plugins. Add anything. Remove anything. No restart.",
